Repository: {{inputs.repo}}
Brief: {{inputs.brief}}

Read AGENTS.md, STATUS.md and the brief with repo.read, then every file the brief's *Reads* list names. Do not edit anything yet.

Answer with JSON only, no prose around it: {"run": "<the brief's number, e.g. 13>", "name": "<a short kebab-case name>", "branch": "run/<number>-<name>", "items": ["<each definition-of-done item, in order, one line each>"], "files": ["<paths you expect to touch>"], "plan": "<how you will take the items, one paragraph>"}

Repository: {{inputs.repo}}
Brief: {{inputs.brief}}

The Mechanic's plan for this brief, before anything is built:
{{steps.read.output}}

Read the plan as the person who wrote the brief. Say whether it should proceed as written or be revised first. Look for three things: a definition-of-done item the plan does not take, a file the brief names that the plan does not touch, and a step whose result could not be checked. Answer with JSON only, no prose around it: {"verdict": "proceed" or "revise", "issues": ["<each problem, one line, naming the item or the file>"]}. A plan with nothing wrong gets proceed and an empty list; padding a review to look thorough is the failure you are checking for.

Brief: {{inputs.brief}}

The reviewer read your plan and asked for a revision. Its issues:
{{steps.review.output.issues}}

Your plan was:
{{steps.read.output.plan}}

Write what the person reads before deciding. The first line is exactly: The reviewer asked for a revision. Then the issues, one per line, each with what you would change. Nothing else; call no tools. Approved, the build goes ahead on the plan as it stands; rejected, you plan again.

Repository: {{inputs.repo}}
Brief: {{inputs.brief}}
Branch: {{steps.read.output.branch}}

The plan:
{{steps.read.output.plan}}

A second model read the plan before you started; its verdict was {{steps.review.output.verdict}}, and its issues, if any, are these — take each into the plan before you begin:
{{steps.review.output.issues}}

The definition of done, in order:
{{steps.read.output.items}}

Implement the brief. Create the branch with git.branch first. Edit with repo.write, run check, read the end of its output, edit again, until check answers ok: true. Commit with git.commit when it is green; if you are told this is your last turn, say plainly what is done and what is not. Answer with what you changed, what check said, and which definition-of-done items are not met, by number.

